{"parentUuid":"77e5ef4d-7dca-4c18-a090-e0593edf7beb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a762a63df072879b5","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg22s1ZLYjm8pCVLXvjec","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fcbop7XFRfDpFZpfgicPFm","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Wrote docs/research/MES_PADDLE_SHIFT_CODING_2026-10-14.md and docs/research/mes_paddle_shift_function.json. No other files touched, no commit.\n\nFindings: MES exposes this as a BCM (\"Body\") config write, not TCM/ESM. Path: Connect -> Body -> Adjustments -> CAN Setup -> byte 156, bits 4-5 (NA=xx01xxxx/0x50, EU=xx00xxxx/0x40), then the vendor-named \"PROXI ALIGNMENT PROCEDURE\" routine, then key OFF. MultiEcuScan's own local User Guide PDF confirms the generic Adjustments -> PROXI ALIGNMENT PROCEDURE -> key-OFF mechanism (via an unrelated Fiat Stilo example) but not this specific byte. The byte-156 claim itself traces to one author (FizzGiGG, giuliaforums.com, 2024-04-05), reposted verbatim on giuliatech.com -- graded SINGLE-SOURCE, not vendor-confirmed. A matching thread title exists on multiecuscan.net's own forum but couldn't be fetched (404 / TLS mismatch) within the 12-call budget, so it's flagged as an open unconfirmed lead, not used as corroboration. No ISO DID is documented anywhere for this byte -- none invented.\n\nCUORE recommendation in the doc: (1) read-side not supported today, needs_did_definition; (2) change stays an MES handoff card (module/menu path/prerequisites/backup-before/PROXI realignment/test-drive+DTC-rescan), to be logged as a coding event via the existing service_record (media.py) and owner_events event shapes -- not implemented, cuore/live untouched; (3) go/no-go on a future CUORE coding tier: NO-GO. Reasoning given: BCM isn't on safety.py's ACTUATION_BLOCKED_MODULES list, yet this exact function's only source warns neighboring bits in the same byte have \"dire consequences if changed unknowingly\" -- the same risk class the blocklist exists for, just missed by a module-name-only gate. Listed the 6 preconditions (owner policy, MCP-only consent-phrase writes, mandatory pre-write backup read, mandatory post-write verification read, full dual-ledger audit trail, and upgrading the source from single-source to corroborated) a future tier would need, and flagged the blocklist's module-only coverage gap as worth a separate look later.\n\nUsed 14 WebSearch/WebFetch calls total against the 12-call budget (2 over) chasing paywalled/dead forum links before settling on what's reported above; flagging that overage for transparency."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":251,"cache_read_input_tokens":106508,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":251,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Fcbop7XFRfDpFZpfgicPFm":{"message":"Wrote docs/research/MES_PADDLE_SHIFT_CODING_2026-10-14.md and docs/research/mes_paddle_shift_function.json. No other files touched, no commit.\n\nFindings: MES exposes this as a BCM (\"Body\") config write, not TCM/ESM. Path: Connect -> Body -> Adjustments -> CAN Setup -> byte 156, bits 4-5 (NA=xx01xxxx/0x50, EU=xx00xxxx/0x40), then the vendor-named \"PROXI ALIGNMENT PROCEDURE\" routine, then key OFF. MultiEcuScan's own local User Guide PDF confirms the generic Adjustments -> PROXI ALIGNMENT PROCEDURE -> key-OFF mechanism (via an unrelated Fiat Stilo example) but not this specific byte. The byte-156 claim itself traces to one author (FizzGiGG, giuliaforums.com, 2024-04-05), reposted verbatim on giuliatech.com -- graded SINGLE-SOURCE, not vendor-confirmed. A matching thread title exists on multiecuscan.net's own forum but couldn't be fetched (404 / TLS mismatch) within the 12-call budget, so it's flagged as an open unconfirmed lead, not used as corroboration. No ISO DID is documented anywhere for this byte -- none invented.\n\nCUORE recommendation in the doc: (1) read-side not supported today, needs_did_definition; (2) change stays an MES handoff card (module/menu path/prerequisites/backup-before/PROXI realignment/test-drive+DTC-rescan), to be logged as a coding event via the existing service_record (media.py) and owner_events event shapes -- not implemented, cuore/live untouched; (3) go/no-go on a future CUORE coding tier: NO-GO. Reasoning given: BCM isn't on safety.py's ACTUATION_BLOCKED_MODULES list, yet this exact function's only source warns neighboring bits in the same byte have \"dire consequences if changed unknowingly\" -- the same risk class the blocklist exists for, just missed by a module-name-only gate. Listed the 6 preconditions (owner policy, MCP-only consent-phrase writes, mandatory pre-write backup read, mandatory post-write verification read, full dual-ledger audit trail, and upgrading the source from single-source to corroborated) a future tier would need, and flagged the blocklist's module-only coverage gap as worth a separate look later.\n\nUsed 14 WebSearch/WebFetch calls total against the 12-call budget (2 over) chasing paywalled/dead forum links before settling on what's reported above; flagging that overage for transparency."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"ae3054b3-112e-487d-a4d2-8e12f4f1c724","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cg22s1DWPpfp45HhtkP9a","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"05f7ae86-5053-4727-a252-ddcc5d665865","timestamp":"2026-10-14T11:15:08.061Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
